    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Armenian is a branch of the Indo-European family on its own; in 1875 Heinrich Hübschmann showed that it is not an Iranian language, despite its many Parthian and Persian loanwords.</li><li>Written since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesrop-mashtots">Mesrop Mashtots</a> created its alphabet in 405, it kept Classical Armenian, or Grabar, as its literary standard from the 5th century to the 19th.</li><li>Two modern standards grew up in the 19th century: Eastern Armenian, the official language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, and Western Armenian, carried by the diaspora after the Armenian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Because Armenian borrowed so much, only around 1,500 words of Classical Armenian are known to have been inherited from Indo-European; even its word for “skull”, xalam, is thought to come from an Anatolian language, cognate with Hittite ḫalanta, “head”.</p></aside>
    </section>

